**Theorem.** Let $S$ be a finite set of distinct integers. Then the elements of $S$ can be arranged in a sequence $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ such that when the integers are placed as points on the real line (at their natural positions) and each consecutive pair $a_i, a_{i+1}$ is joined by a semicircular arc in the open upper half-plane, no two arcs cross (intersect in their interiors).

**Proof.**

Let $S = \{s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_n\}$ and sort the elements so that $s_1 < s_2 < \cdots < s_n$. We claim the arrangement $a_i = s_i$ (the sorted order) works.

For each $i \in \{1, 2, \ldots, n-1\}$, draw the semicircular arc $A_i$ in the upper half-plane with diameter $[s_i, s_{i+1}]$, connecting the point $s_i$ to the point $s_{i+1}$.

We show no two arcs $A_i$ and $A_j$ with $i < j$ cross. There are two cases:

**Case 1: $j \geq i + 2$.** Then $s_{i+1} < s_j$ (strict inequality, since the $s_k$ are distinct integers and $i+1 < j$). The arc $A_i$ lies above the interval $[s_i, s_{i+1}]$ and the arc $A_j$ lies above the interval $[s_j, s_{j+1}]$. Since $s_{i+1} < s_j$, these two intervals on the real line are disjoint. Two semicircular arcs in the upper half-plane over disjoint intervals on the real line share no points whatsoever. Hence $A_i$ and $A_j$ do not intersect.

**Case 2: $j = i + 1$.** The arcs $A_i$ and $A_{i+1}$ share the endpoint $s_{i+1}$ on the real line. The arc $A_i$ lies above $[s_i, s_{i+1}]$ and the arc $A_{i+1}$ lies above $[s_{i+1}, s_{i+2}]$. These two intervals share only the single point $s_{i+1}$. The two semicircular arcs meet only at this shared endpoint $s_{i+1}$ (which lies on the real line, the boundary of the upper half-plane) and do not cross in the interior of the upper half-plane. Specifically, $A_i$ approaches $s_{i+1}$ from the left and $A_{i+1}$ departs from $s_{i+1}$ to the right; they do not cross.

In both cases, the arcs do not cross. Since this holds for every pair $i < j$, the entire collection of arcs is pairwise non-crossing.

Therefore, the sorted arrangement $s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_n$ yields a non-intersecting configuration. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark.** The key observation is that sorting the integers places consecutive elements adjacently on the line, so the arcs connect only neighboring points. Neighboring arcs share at most an endpoint (never crossing), and non-neighboring arcs lie over disjoint intervals (never meeting at all). The discreteness of integers ensures strict separation: $s_{i+1} < s_j$ whenever $i+1 < j$, since distinct integers differ by at least $1$.

